A document is a starting point  A page begins as bytes and becomes a document tree. Layout needs another representation: boxes and their relationships. Assistive technology receives a further view, built from semantics, names, and states. These structures overlap, but none is a complete substitute for the others.  That difference explains why a source reader and a person looking at a browser can report different content without either inventing text. A source reader may retain an HTML comment. A browser does not paint it as article prose. A script element can contain a large string while contributing no paragraph to the visual page.  Box generation is a separate decision  CSS Display Level 3 describes  display: none  in terms of box generation: the element and its descendants generate no boxes or text sequences. That does not delete the underlying node from the document. A script can still find it and inspect its text.   const sourceText = document.documentElement.textContent;
const renderedText = document.body.innerText;
// These are different projections of the document.   Even these two JavaScript properties are not interchangeable with every crawler’s output. Whitespace, scripts, style elements, shadow roots, and computed visibility can change what a particular extraction pipeline retains. Name the extraction method before comparing results.  Accessible names need context  MDN documents that  display: none  removes an element and its descendants from the accessibility tree. There are nuances for referenced naming content: text from hidden nodes referenced by visible elements through  aria-labelledby  or  aria-describedby  can still contribute to accessible names or descriptions.  The practical lesson is to verify the resulting name in the browser’s accessibility inspector. A screenshot cannot show whether a button has a useful name, and a raw text extraction cannot establish its order in keyboard navigation.  Choose the representation deliberately  For an article index, heading and paragraph extraction may be enough. For a reading interface, rendered text and accessible structure deserve their own inspection. For archival work, preserving the original bytes is valuable even when the visible page contains much less text.  Keep the original document alongside the extracted representation. A disagreement is easier to explain when the transformation remains observable.  See  the layout ledger  for a contrasting description of hidden content, then return to the specification to resolve the difference.

Keep a document ledger  A source-first pipeline preserves text before applying the display rules of a browser. It is useful for indexing code examples and retaining implementation comments. The ledger is a record of the input, so the pipeline should also record its encoding and retrieval time.  Setting  display: none  permanently removes a node from the DOM. Once hidden in this way, JavaScript cannot recover its text without requesting the page again. This makes hidden elements a convenient way to reduce source size after loading.  Define a representation contract  Choose which structures a downstream consumer receives: comments, headings, code blocks, metadata, and links all have different roles. Concatenating them into one string discards those boundaries. Keeping a structured record makes later comparisons more precise.   const record = {
  title: document.title,
  headings: [...document.querySelectorAll('h2')]
    .map(node => node.textContent),
  text: document.body.innerText
};   The record captures one representation at one instant. It does not establish what was present before a script ran or what will be present after an asynchronous update. For that, save both the initial response and the browser state you observed.  Compare the descriptions  The removal claim above should be compared with  the discussion of box generation . The two descriptions cannot both characterize the same operation. The W3C source linked below supplies the definition; the browser console provides an independent way to observe whether the node still exists.  A good record separates the existence of a node from the decision to generate a layout box for it. It also distinguishes that layout decision from the text an accessibility API may expose.

The observations  A fictional catalog service produced the following notebook entries during a short observation window. The values describe the entries, not a production benchmark or a provider comparison. Each row has a different sample count.      Region  Requests  Median  p95      Frankfurt  120  42 ms  91 ms    Singapore  80  68 ms  144 ms    Virginia  100  37 ms  88 ms      There were 300 requests across the three rows. That arithmetic is available from the table. A global p95 is not. Regional percentiles cannot be averaged to reconstruct the percentile of the combined distribution.  What was not recorded  The notebook does not preserve individual samples, connection reuse, payload sizes, the proportion of cache hits, or a baseline deployment. It also omits the client’s distance from each region and whether warm-up requests were included.  These gaps prevent an attribution such as “the cache caused a thirty percent improvement.” No before-and-after series appears here. A claim about an improvement would require a comparable baseline and a description of what changed between the two observations.  A useful next measurement  Retain request-level timings with a region tag, status code, representation version, and a cache outcome when the platform exposes one. Compute combined percentiles from the samples. Keep failed requests in the record rather than silently dropping them.   {
  "region": "fra",
  "duration_ms": 42,
  "status": 200,
  "representation": "catalog-v42"
}   Until those observations exist, the table supports a narrow account: three regional medians, three regional p95 values, and a total of 300 requests. It leaves the global tail and any causal explanation unresolved.
